course: medicine
lesson: 62
title: The Olympics and Paralympics
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Summer Olympics come every four years, Paris 2024, Los Angeles 2028, Brisbane 2032, with the Winter Games two years in between, and the Paralympics follow straight after each one in the same city.
explain: Zara in Croydon wants to be in it, and her dad says «Get training, our kid» (Northern for my child or mate): every athlete there has spent years training muscles, heart and lungs, and even the greatest still lose some races.
explain: The Paralympic movement grew from the 1948 Stoke Mandeville Games in England, started by Dr Ludwig Guttmann for injured Second World War servicemen; the first Paralympics were in Rome in 1960.
explain: Paralympians are grouped into classes by their kind of impairment so races are fair, which is why one distance has several finals; running blades spring back, but the runner's own muscles, balance and heart do the work, and in goalball everyone wears eyeshades and follows a ball with bells inside.
fact: The Summer Olympics come every 4 years: Paris 2024, Los Angeles 2028 and Brisbane 2032.
fact: The Tokyo Games were put back a year to 2021 because of the Covid-19 pandemic but kept the name «2020», and most events were held without crowds.
fact: An Olympic gold medal is mostly silver with a coating of gold; the rules say at least 6 g of gold.
fact: The five rings stand for the inhabited continents coming together, but no ring officially belongs to one continent.
fact: Usain Bolt ran 100 m in 9.58 seconds in 2009, still the world record, about 10.4 metres every second.
fact: Michael Phelps won 28 Olympic medals, 23 gold, 3 silver and 2 bronze, the most of anyone, so even he lost some races.
fact: The Paralympic movement began with the 1948 Stoke Mandeville Games, and the first Paralympics were in Rome in 1960.
fact: Wheelchair rugby, once nicknamed «murderball», was invented in Winnipeg, Canada, in 1977, and later became a Paralympic sport.
fact: Paralympians are put into classes by the kind and level of their impairment, so one distance can have several finals.
fact: Carbon-fibre running blades spring back, but the athlete's muscles, balance and heart still do the work, and it takes years to learn to run on them.
fact: Goalball is played 3 against 3 by athletes with visual impairments; everyone wears eyeshades so it is fair, and the ball has bells inside.
fact: The Winter Olympics in Milan and Cortina, Italy, were in February 2026; skaters balance using the inner ear, eyes and strong leg and core muscles.
fact: «Hen» is a Glasgow word for a girl or woman, «eish» is a South African word of surprise, and a «toque» is a Canadian woolly hat.
exercise: Zara asks when the next Summer Olympics are. Which city in 2028? | Los Angeles | Brisbane | Paris | Rio de Janeiro
exercise: After 2028, where are the 2032 Summer Olympics? | Brisbane | Los Angeles | Rome | Toronto
exercise: Liam: «Why's it called Tokyo 2020 if it was on in 2021?» | It was postponed a year by Covid-19 | It was cancelled and replaced | Japan uses a different calendar from ours | The name was a printing mistake
exercise: Thandi bites the medal: «Lekker, real gold!» What's it mostly made of? | Silver with gold on the outside | Solid real gold all the way through | Bronze painted gold to look shiny | Plastic with gold paint on the top
exercise: Ellie: «the blue one's Europe, innit?» What's true about the rings? | No ring officially belongs to one continent | Blue is officially Europe | Each colour is one country that took part first | The rings stand for five sports
exercise: Jayden runs 100 m in 18 seconds. How much slower than Bolt's 9.58? | 8.42 seconds | 9.58 seconds | 18 seconds | 0.42 seconds
exercise: Hannah: «Phelps never lost a race, eh?» How many of his 28 medals were not gold? | 5 | 0 | 23 | 2
exercise: Josh: «Wheelchair rugby's from round here, Stoke Mandeville». Where was it really invented? | Winnipeg, Canada | Stoke Mandeville, England | Rome, Italy | Sydney, Australia
exercise: What did start at Stoke Mandeville in 1948? | The games that grew into the Paralympics | The first Winter Olympics | Wheelchair rugby | The first World Cup
exercise: Where and when were the first Paralympics? | Rome, 1960 | London, 1948 | Tokyo, 2020 | Paris, 1924
exercise: Ruby: «So many 100 m finals at the Paralympics, that's sus». Why are there several? | Athletes race in classes by impairment | Too many people wanted medals, so they split them | Some runners didn't finish | They run each race twice
exercise: Kai: «The blades do all the running, aye?» What's true? | The runner's muscles and balance do the work | The blades run on their own | Blades are springs you just stand on | Anyone wins with blades
exercise: Anaya: «Why's the ball jingling?» In goalball? | It has bells so players can hear it | It's there to annoy the other team's fans | It counts the goals as they are scored | It means the ball is brand new from the box
exercise: Why does everyone in goalball wear eyeshades? | So it's fair whatever their sight | To keep sweat out of their eyes during play | To look scary to opponents | Because the lights are bright
exercise: When were the Milan and Cortina Winter Olympics? | February 2026 | July 2024 | February 2022 | August 2028
exercise: Noah: «Hockey's the only sport that matters, eh». What do skaters need to stay balanced? | Inner ear, eyes and strong legs and core | Only sharp skates and a good coach | Only good luck | Nothing much, ice is easy once you're moving
test: Zara: «The Olympics are every year, right?» Correct her. | Summer Games every 4 years, Winter 2 years later | Every year in a new city | Every 10 years | Only when a country asks
test: Liam's mate: «Tokyo 2020 was cancelled, so it was». What really happened? | Postponed a year, held mostly without crowds | Cancelled and never held | Moved to London in 2020 | Held in 2019 instead
test: Thandi's cousin says «Eish, it's mostly silver, sis». How much gold must a gold medal have at least? | 6 g | 500 g | 1 kg | None at all
test: Jayden's sister: «Bruv. You're nearly twice as slow». Is she right about 18 seconds against 9.58? | Yes, 18 is nearly twice 9.58 | No, he's only a bit slower | No, he's faster than Bolt | Yes, he's exactly 5 times slower
test: What would happen if a goalball ball had no bells? | Players couldn't track it by sound | It would bounce much higher off the floor | It would be much easier to save every shot | Nothing, they just look at it as it rolls
test: Josh is right about one thing. Which? | Stoke Mandeville is where the Paralympic movement began | Wheelchair rugby was invented in Aylesbury | Paralympics started in 2012 | Murderball was an Olympic sport
test: Ruby's Da: «Not sus at all, wee one». Why is a class system fair? | Athletes race others with similar impairments | It gives everyone a medal | It slows the fastest runners down so races are close | It lets the crowd choose winners
test: A cartoon says «Paralympians aren't proper athletes». What's true? | They train full time for years like Olympians | They train only on weekends | They don't have coaches | Their races aren't timed
test: Anaya's mum says «Watch, hen». What does «hen» mean in Glasgow? | A girl or woman, like «love» | A chicken | A goal | A referee
test: Kai's cousin: «Blades are cheating, you just bounce». What would happen to someone with no training on blades? | They'd struggle even to stand still | They'd beat Bolt straight away on their first try | They'd float above the track | They'd never get tired
test: Hannah's brother: «The greatest never lose». How does Phelps show he's wrong? | Even Phelps won silver and bronze too | Phelps won every single race he ever swam | Phelps never swam a final | Phelps lost every race
exam: Identify the interval at which the Summer Olympic Games are ordinarily held. | Every four years | Every single year | Every two years in summer | Once every ten years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of an Olympic gold medal? | It consists principally of silver with a coating of gold | It consists entirely of gold | It consists principally of bronze | It contains no precious metal whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the origin of the Paralympic movement. | The Stoke Mandeville Games of 1948 | The Tokyo Games of 2020, after the pandemic | The Winnipeg Games of 1977 | The Paris Games of 2024
exam: It is given that several finals are contested over a single distance at the Paralympic Games. Identify the principal reason. | Athletes are classified according to impairment | Athletes are classified according to nationality | Athletes are classified according to height | Athletes are classified according to age alone
